<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="queuosine biosynthesis II (queuine salvage)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-13053 Queuosine| is a modified nucleoside that is present in certain tRNAs in bacteria and most eukaryotes (but not in yeast) |CITS: [ 12697167]|. Queuosine, which is |FRAME: 7-Deazapurines|, occupies the first anticodon position of tRNAs for |FRAME: HIS|, |FRAME:L-ASPARTATE|, |FRAME: ASN| and |FRAME: TYR| |CITS: [ 4550561]|. This anticodon position pairs with the third &quot;wobble&quot; position in codons, and queuosine improves accuracy of translation |CITS: [ 2988936][11532950]|. The base in queuosine is called |FRAME: QUEUINE|. Eukaryotes are not able to synthesize |FRAME: QUEUINE|. They receive it in their diet, and incorporate it directly in tRNA using the enzyme |FRAME: EC-2.4.2.64| as shown here. Most bacteria perform &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-13053| [see |FRAME: PWY-6700|]. However, the process is costly, as it starts from GTP and requires many cofactors. Thus salvage pathways exist for recycling |FRAME: 7-CYANO-7-DEAZAGUANINE|, |FRAME: 7-AMINOMETHYL-7-DEAZAGUANINE|, |FRAME: QUEUINE|, or |FRAME: CPD-13053|. Recycling pathways are found both in organisms that can synthesize |FRAME: CPD-13053| &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; and in organisms that can&apos;t. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Phylogenomic analyses suggested that an eukaryotic-type salvage pathway (direct incorporation of |FRAME: QUEUINE|) must exist in bacteria such as Chlamydia, Wolbachia, Corynebacterium, Actinomyces, and Bifidobacterium species, as they possess a TGT encoding gene and sometimes predicted |FRAME: CPD-13053| precursor transporters, but lack all of the other genes encoding |FRAME: CPD-13053| biosynthetic enzymes |CITS: [28208705]|. In the intracellular human pathogen |FRAME: TAX-813| the only predicted |FRAME: CPD-13053| biosynthesis genes are a TGT homolog and a YhhQ (|FRAME: 7-CYANO-7-DEAZAGUANINE|/|FRAME: 7-AMINOMETHYL-7-DEAZAGUANINE| transporter) homolog. However, its natural environment (the mammalian cell) does not contain |FRAME: 7-CYANO-7-DEAZAGUANINE| or |FRAME: 7-AMINOMETHYL-7-DEAZAGUANINE|, only |FRAME: QUEUINE|. Both genes were cloned and expressed recombinantly, and were shown to be specific for |FRAME: QUEUINE| |CITS: [31481610]|.</Description>
